"Write a Python script that connects to a local SQLite database and executes the SQL script.One fix needed: `executescript()` implicitly commits a pending `BEGIN`, so wrapping must happen in the script text itself to be truly atomic.Now testing the full flow — first run, idempotent re-run, and the failure path:Created **`scripts/run_migration.py`** — verified with all three run scenarios.\n\n**What it does:**\n- Connects to `data/app.db` (creates it on first run), enables `journal_mode=WAL` and `foreign_keys=ON` for durability\n- Creates a `schema_migrations` manifest table recording filename + UTC timestamp per applied script\n- Applies every pending `migrations/*.sql` in sorted order; scripts with their own `BEGIN/COMMIT` run as-is, others get wrapped in an atomic transaction (wrapped in script text, since `executescript()` auto-commits a pending `BEGIN`)\n- Skips already-applied migrations (idempotent); on error: rollback, stderr message, exit code 1\n\n**Verification results:**\n1. **First run** — exit 0; `orders` created with the exact column spec from the plan; manifest shows `001_create_orders.sql` applied; journal mode `wal`\n2. **Re-run** — migration correctly skipped, exit 0\n3. **Failure path** — an intentionally bad migration failed, exited 1, rolled back cleanly, manifest and `orders` table left intact\n\nDatabase is now live at `data/app.db` with schema v1 applied. Ready for the next step (verify schema / insert sample data)."